Docker "separate Linux host" for Inferno protocol interop testing
================================================================

This image is a small Linux host running real, mainstream server software:

  - OpenSSH sshd
  - Postfix, SMTP submission over implicit TLS (SMTPS, port 465), SASL auth
  - Dovecot, IMAP over implicit TLS (IMAPS, port 993)

all authenticating a single account ('tester'). It exists so the Inferno
client - running on the host, unchanged - can be pointed at it to prove that
Inferno actually speaks these protocols to independent implementations, not
to a stub. The container is the server side only; no emu runs inside it, so
it builds and runs on the host's native architecture (no emulation).

The whole thing is driven by tests/mailsshinterop.sh, which:

  - generates a throwaway CA + server cert (for mail.test.local), an sshd host
    key, and an ssh client key, and drops them in a /shared volume the
    container reads;
  - runs this container with 22/465/993 published to the loopback;
  - temporarily trusts the test CA in this tree's TLS bundle (lib/tls) so the
    Inferno TLS client accepts the server cert, and restores it afterwards;
  - drives the Inferno clients against the container:
      * ssh(1): publickey login + a remote command (and an unauthorised-key
        negative control);
      * upas/send over smtp2(2): compose and send a message to Postfix;
      * upas/fetch over imap4(2): fetch it back from Dovecot;
    the headline check is the full round trip - a message SENT by Inferno's
    SMTP client and FETCHED back by Inferno's IMAP client through the real
    servers - plus a wrong-password negative control on IMAP.

Run it (from the repo root):

    docker build -t inferno-interop-host tests/docker
    sh tests/mailsshinterop.sh

A successful run ends with:

    INTEROP: ALL PASS - Inferno ssh/SMTP/IMAP interoperate with OpenSSH/Postfix/Dovecot

Files:
  Dockerfile - the server image (Debian + OpenSSH + Postfix + Dovecot).
  start.sh   - container entrypoint: configures the three daemons from the
               cert/host-key/authorized_keys in /shared, then launches them.

The client transport is the same one the Gmail work uses (tls12(2)/https(2)
under imap4(2)/smtp2(2)); tests/upasmail.sh exercises the mail client against
a Python TLS stub, and this test raises that to real Postfix/Dovecot plus ssh.
